## Lesson catalog — bugs already hit here

### Design tokens and Tailwind v4

- **Import order in `frontend-web/app/globals.css` is load-bearing.** The generated
  tokens declare the `--ds-*` properties; the adapter maps them onto the names
  Tailwind's `@theme` reads. Reverse the two and every `var(--ds-*)` in the
  adapter points at a property that does not exist yet — which CSS resolves to
  nothing, **silently**, producing a page with no colours and no error anywhere.
- **Clearing a namespace (`--spacing-*: initial`) also removes Tailwind's bare
  multiplier**, and that is the point rather than a side effect. With the
  multiplier alive, `p-<n>` resolves for *any* n, so `p-7` renders a value
  belonging to no scale. Without it, only registered roles produce a utility and
  anything else produces nothing at all.
- **`--spacing-<key>` is consulted BEFORE `--container-<key>` for the whole
  width family** (`w-`, `max-w-`, `min-w-`, `basis-`) — undocumented, widely
  reported as
  [tailwindlabs/tailwindcss#16047](https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/issues/16047).
  A design system with `sm`/`md`/`lg` spacing keys silently hijacks `max-w-md`
  into a 40px value, which shows up as an element collapsing to zero width, not
  as anything that looks like a scale problem. This repo is immune by
  construction: spacing roles are named by job (`stack-tight`, `section-gap`),
  and a job name cannot collide with a container name. **Keep it that way** —
  the day someone adds `--spacing-md`, the bug returns.
- **`z-10` and `z-50` survive anything the theme does.** Tailwind builds bare
  numeric `z-*` arithmetically without consulting the theme, so clearing the
  namespace does not remove them and named layers only get added alongside. CSS
  cannot close this; the ESLint rule `no-unnamed-z-index-class` is the only
  place left where it can be closed.
- **No class-facing name may resolve to a primitive** — the invariant the whole
  token architecture rests on. The adapter is the only place a token is given a
  class name, so it is the only place the boundary can be broken; register
  `--color-x: var(--ds-color-neutral-700)` and the project has `bg-x` forever.
  The generator checks this on every run against the exact list of variables the
  primitive tiers emitted, **because role and primitive cannot be told apart by
  name shape** — both compile to `--ds-color-*`.
- **A contrast decision must be made against the worst surface, not the
  obvious one.** Muted text is tightest on the *inset* surface, which is the
  lightest of the dark set, not on the darkest one. Measuring against the
  darkest gives an answer that is comfortably wrong.
- **Contrast thresholds move with font size, so the type scale and the palette
  are one problem, not two.** APCA drops its requirement at 15px and again at
  18px; the original 11–32 scale sat on the strict side of both jumps, which
  made three distinguishable levels of grey text arithmetically impossible. The
  fix was the type scale (now 15–36), not the colours.

### React, Server Components and hydration

- **A `"use client"` module is an opaque client reference, and only its
  COMPONENT exports cross the boundary correctly.** A plain constant exported
  from one arrives as `undefined` on the server — so server code reading a
  storage key from a provider module calls `localStorage.getItem(undefined)`,
  reads a key literally named `"undefined"`, and breaks with no error anywhere.
  Constants shared across the boundary go in a **directive-free file** of their
  own (`theme/provider/constants.ts`); that file's lack of `"use client"` is
  load-bearing, not untidiness.
- **State that lives outside React is read with `useSyncExternalStore`, not
  copied in with an effect.** The first version of the theme provider used an
  effect after mount. That does remove the hydration mismatch, but it costs a
  full re-render of the tree and leaves the wrong theme on screen until it runs.
  `useSyncExternalStore` exists for exactly this shape: server snapshot during
  hydration, live value immediately after, with React aware the two differ on
  purpose.
- **Nothing a test can wait for corresponds to a React effect.** `networkidle`
  tracks the network and nothing else. Any correctness that depends on an effect
  having run is therefore untestable *and* user-visible as a flash — which is
  why the theme is applied by a synchronous inline `<script>` in `<head>` that
  runs before hydration and before the first paint. The root element needs
  `suppressHydrationWarning` alongside it, because the script mutates the DOM
  before React compares it to the server's output.
- **Strict Mode remounts in development and resets the root element's class
  list to what React manages**, wiping what that inline script set. A
  `useLayoutEffect` restores it before the browser paints; a passive effect lets
  the wrong theme flash back in. This is the one place an effect is right —
  writing a class onto an element React does not own is a side effect, not
  derived state.
- A cookie read on the server was considered for theme and rejected: it opts the
  route out of static rendering, and cookie-varying HTML in a shared cache is
  how one reader's preference gets served to everyone.

### Vitest and Testing Library

- **`fireEvent.click`'s default `MouseEvent.detail: 0` is indistinguishable
  from a real Enter/Space key activation to code that branches on it** (Base
  UI's menu internals do — a real mouse click highlights nothing until an
  arrow key is pressed, a keyboard-equivalent activation pre-highlights the
  first item). A jsdom test asserting "click behavior" can therefore silently
  exercise the keyboard path instead — caught only because a Playwright run
  against real Chromium disagreed with the passing jsdom test. Pass
  `{ detail: 1 }` explicitly when the distinction matters.

### Playwright and the accessibility suites

- **`webServer.url` must point at a route that actually exists.** Probing `/`
  when the app has no root page means the readiness check 404s until it times
  out, and the failure reads like a build failure, sending you to debug the
  wrong thing entirely.
- **Seed state with `addInitScript` before `goto`, never by waiting.**
  `addInitScript` runs ahead of every script the page owns, including the
  pre-hydration one. A suite that instead waits for the provider's effects lands
  inside that window at random and produces a flake that looks exactly like a
  visual regression.
- **One APCA threshold for all text is wrong** and produces a wall of false
  failures that discredits the whole check. APCA levels are per use case — body
  text, prominent text, spot-readable — and a disabled control is *supposed* to
  sit at the bottom one.
- **axe `incomplete` is not a pass.** A pair axe could not measure is an unknown
  pair, not a passing one, and it stays silent unless the spec fails on it
  explicitly.
- **Two specs attaching results under the same attachment name collide** in the
  reporter and become indistinguishable. WCAG and APCA are deliberately separate
  suites — so either can be removed without touching the other — and they need
  separate attachment names to stay that way.
- **`exactOptionalPropertyTypes` forbids assigning `undefined`** to an optional
  property. Use conditional spreading (`...(cond ? { key: value } : {})`) rather
  than assigning `undefined` and hoping.
- **`playwright test --update-snapshots <pattern>` silently eats `<pattern>`
  as the flag's own optional `[mode]` argument** — commander.js (`--update-snapshots`
  accepts `all|changed|missing|none`) greedily binds the very next token,
  so `<pattern>` never reaches the test filter and instead fails with
  `argument '<pattern>' is invalid` ([microsoft/playwright#36297](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/issues/36297)).
  Put the positional pattern before the flag: `playwright test <pattern>
  --update-snapshots`. Omitting the mode value keeps the default `changed`
  behaviour (rewrite mismatches, create missing) — the fix is only ordering,
  not a semantic change.

### CI, GitHub Actions and baseline scripts

- **A path-shaped string is not the same claim as an existing path.**
  `baseline-request-guard.mjs`'s `MODULE_PATTERN` regex only rejects shell
  metacharacters; it happily accepts `storybook` (the package.json `name`,
  read off `packages/storybook/README.md`'s own `pnpm --filter
  tallyvane-storybook` examples) as if it were the actual directory
  `packages/storybook`. The guard job's checkout is sparse and pinned to the
  default branch, so it cannot `fs.existsSync` its way to a real answer —
  existence has to be checked against the pull request's own tree, over the
  Contents API by `head.sha`, not the file system. Left unchecked, the
  malformed value survives all the way to `working-directory: storybook` in
  the next job and fails there as a bare `bash: No such file or directory`,
  three jobs removed from the actual mistake.

### Lint, architecture rules and the toolchain

- **pnpm resolves a workspace package as a symlink to its source, not into
  `node_modules`.** A dependency-cruiser rule matching `node_modules/<pkg>`
  therefore never fires — the rule looks correct, reports nothing, and gives
  false confidence. Match the relative source path instead, and always verify a
  new rule by deliberately breaking it once.
- **ESLint 10:** `eslint-plugin-import` does not support it; use
  `eslint-plugin-import-x`, the maintained fork.
- **`eslint-plugin-boundaries` v7** renamed most of its config: `element-types`
  → `dependencies`, `rules` → `policies`, `mode: "full"` → `partialMatch: false`,
  and string selectors → objects. The old shape still runs, emitting a wall of
  deprecation warnings rather than failing.
- **Type-aware rules must be scoped to files a tsconfig actually covers.**
  Applying `recommendedTypeChecked` project-wide makes the flat config's own
  `.mjs` files explode, since nothing type-checks them.
- **Steiger:** an empty FSD segment fails `fsd/public-api` — give it an
  `index.ts`, not a `.gitkeep`. `providers` is a banned segment name.
- **Generated output must be excluded from every checker**, not just from git:
  the compiled token tree, `.next/`, `playwright-report/`, `test-results/`.
- **The FSD layer cannot be named `pages/`** — it collides with Next.js's legacy
  Pages Router auto-detection. This repo uses `views/`, applied at scaffolding
  time; do not "fix" it back.

### Local development on Windows

- The shell is **PowerShell**, not bash. `&&` works in modern PowerShell but
  quoting rules differ; paths containing spaces need double quotes.
- A dev server that accepts connections but never responds to any request,
  including static ones, after many rapid restarts is stale process or socket
  state, not a code bug. Kill all `node` processes, clear `.next/`, retry on a
  fresh port before assuming the code broke.

